 {"id": "CF-354", "title": "The scheduler sends text_b64 and the in-repo node reads text — this repo's node cannot execute a single dispatch it is sent", "status": "pending", "priority": "P1", "complexity": "medium", "reasoning": "Field-by-field comparison of specs/ENVELOPE.md against both implementations (docs/master/37, VERIFIED): 23 of 24 spec fields are wrong on at least one side. The sharpest: the scheduler emits `text_b64` while scripts/node-bus-worker.py reads `text`, so a dispatch from the scheduler is unexecutable by the node in this repo. The live fleet works only because ~/crier-fleet/dispatcher.py decodes text_b64 — i.e. the working path is outside the repository the docs describe.", "acceptance_criteria": "1. Sender and receiver agree on the field name and the encoding, proven by one real dispatch consumed by the in-repo node.\n2. The spec states the encoding rule rather than leaving `text` undefined.\n3. A fixture dispatch from the scheduler runs end to end through node-bus-worker.py.", "created_at": "2026-11-05T20:30:54Z", "updated_at": "2026-11-05T20:30:54Z"}
 {"id": "CF-355", "title": "The scheduler never reads any of the 12 result fields, and a failed result with no `ok` is recorded as success", "status": "pending", "priority": "P1", "complexity": "medium", "reasoning": "docs/master/37 measured all 12 result fields as unread: the correlator matches only in_reply_to/corr_id/task.corr_id. Worse, dispatch_leg.go:442 treats an ABSENT `ok` as success, so a result carrying status: failed or refused correlates on corr_id and is then read as a pass. The live dispatcher happens to send `ok`, which is why the fleet escapes it.", "acceptance_criteria": "1. The scheduler reads status/ok from every result and refuses a result that carries neither.\n2. A fixture posting status=failed with no ok is recorded as FAIL, never success.\n3. The result field set is agreed with crier and validated on receipt.", "created_at": "2026-11-05T20:30:54Z", "updated_at": "2026-11-05T20:30:54Z"}
 {"id": "CF-356", "title": "configure_family configures exactly ONE lane against a real daemon, fails fifteen, and exits 0", "status": "pending", "priority": "P1", "complexity": "medium", "reasoning": "Measured against a scratch schedulerd built from source on a copied DB: the script opens a single SQLite write transaction at its first UPDATE and commits after the loop, so the daemon's INSERT for the next lane returns `500 database is locked (SQLITE_BUSY)` after ~10 s. A real run therefore configures the first lane in sorted order, fails 15, and exits 0 (run 1: 170 s, run 2: 150 s). This corrects the earlier '16 configured, 6 created' reading, which came from a stub HTTP layer that had no locking.", "acceptance_criteria": "1. Each lane's write is its own transaction, or the transaction is held only across the API calls, not the DB writes.\n2. A busy/locked failure is retried with backoff and reported per lane.\n3. A run that configured fewer lanes than it targeted exits non-zero and names the lanes.", "created_at": "2026-11-05T20:30:54Z", "updated_at": "2026-11-05T20:30:54Z"}
